A binary wrong result rejects the complete proposed rule, not just a guessed cell. Before another request or submission, revisit all demos and partition the hypothesis into (1) trigger, (2) selected object or stroke extent, and (3) replacement color. Record exactly which cells change and which similar motifs remain untouched. Do not let a familiar visual analogy override these constraints.

For thin strokes among larger color masses, test the relation of each candidate stroke to mass boundaries and endpoints. A stroke color may be replaced by a mass color without changing the mass itself, while visually similar strokes can remain unchanged if the trigger is more specific. Verify each proposed trigger and transformed cell against every demo. A wrong first candidate is reason to rebuild the mapping, not to repeat the same theory or request another example without a concrete discriminating question.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/post-rejection-extent-color-audit.md"}
